 "drivingQuestion": "Does the mishna's 'if he reduced them it is fit' resolve the dichui dilemma?",
 "outcome": "inconclusive",
 "outcomeNote": "the proposed resolution is rejected in the next unit",
 "continuesQuestionOf": "sukkah.sugya104",
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "sukkah.33b.4",
   "daf": "33b",
   "sentenceId": 3,
   "he": "אִם מִיעֲטָן — כָּשֵׁר. דְּמַעֲטִינְהוּ אֵימַת? אִילֵּימָא מִקַּמֵּיה דְּלֹאגְדֵיהּ — פְּשִׁיטָא! אֶלָּא לְבָתַר דְּלֹאגְדֵיהּ — דָּחוּי מֵעִיקָּרָא הוּא! תִּפְשׁוֹט מִינַּהּ: דָּחוּי מֵעִיקָּרָא — לָא הָוֵי דָּחוּי!",
   "en": "§ The mishna continues: «If he diminished their» number, «it is fit.» The Gemara asks: This is a case «where he diminished their» number «when? If you say» that he did so «before he bound» the lulav, it is «obvious» that it is fit. When he performs the mitzva with it, the leaves outnumber the berries. «Rather,» it must be that he diminished their number «after he bound» the lulav with the other species. If so, «it is» a case of «disqualification from the outset,» as it was unfit at the time that it was bound. «Resolve from here» the dilemma that was raised and conclude that «disqualification from the outset is not» permanent «disqualification.»"
  }
 ],
